</mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mrow data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">x</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn><mrow data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">⊤</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mrow data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">X</mi></mrow><mrow data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">⊤</mi></mrow></msup><mrow data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">X</mi></mrow><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mrow data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mrow data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">x</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container></span> Interpret: the estimate moves in proportion to the new case’s\nprediction error. This is the basis of <em>recursive least squares</em>;\n<a href=\"../ch10-computation/index.html\">Chapter 10</a> treats the\nnumerically stable version.</p>","file":"ch05-model-and-least-squares/04-least-squares.html"}